  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 155 AM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nMain forecast concern this morning will be the snow chances today. \nAn upper-level shortwave and a low-level jet moving into the region \nboth will provide support for snow development during this event. \nHigh-resolution guidance has shown the potential for the \nprecipitation to kick off early this morning over north central \nSouth Dakota, which is a bit sooner than previous forecasts had \nmentioned. This early snow will face a low-level dry layer in the \nlowest kilometer, so with fairly light snow rates initially expected \nthere may be a struggle to saturate the profile. Chances will then \nshift to be over northeastern South Dakota and western Minnesota \nthrough the morning time frame, settling firmly over the region by \nthis afternoon. Snow is expected to linger over that area through \nthe rest of the day and perhaps into the early overnight period.\n\nAs far as snowfall amounts go, confidence has increased in totals \naround half an inch over parts of northeastern South Dakota and \nwestern Minnesota. Latest REFS guidance gives around a 50-80% chance \nfor half an inch of snow over that area, and a 10-30% chance for an \ninch along and east of I-29. NBM probabilities are much less \nconfident (10-30% chance for half an inch), but in general has \nlagged behind many models as this event has approached (leading \nforecaster confidence to lean towards the REFS solution). There is \nalso a question of how much ground temperatures will play a role \nduring this event, as they are still above freezing. This adds to \nthe uncertainty as the warm ground may limit some of the \naccumulation totals. Still, some slick roadways may be possible \nduring the Wednesday morning commute. \n\nOver the next few days, height rises in the form of a weak ridge \nwill move overhead, allowing for a warming trend over the region. By \nFriday, 850mb temperatures peak in the 5-10 Celsius range, roughly \ncentered around the 90th percentile for late November. As a result, \nhighs in the upper 40s to 50s will be possible by the end of the \nwork week, roughly 10-15 degrees above normal. \n\nA low pressure center to the north will drag a cold front across the \nregion Friday. Behind it, winds turn westerly and increase, gusting \nupwards of 30-40 miles per hour west of the Missouri River during \nthe afternoon. Latest NBM guidance resolves a roughly 10-40% chance \nto see Wind Advisory criteria met (45 miles per hour or higher) for \nthat area. These probabilities are have held steady over the past 24 \nhours, but will continue to keep an eye on trends.",
